1. Rh6+ {The rook gives check.} Ke7 {The king, at his second square.} 2. Ke5 {The king, at the
adverse king's fourth square.} Kd7 {The king, at his queen's second square. (a) NOTE: His king
must be opposite to yours, in order that you may give check with your rook; because then his
king is forced, by the check, to retrograde.} 3. Rg6 {The rook, at the adverse king's knight's
third square. (b) NOTE: If you had played your king opposite to his, he would have restored his
king to the same place; whereas at this move he plays his king to the face of yours: you may
then force him back by the check of your rook.} Kc7 {The king, at his queen's bishop's second
square.} 4. Kd5 {The king, at the adverse queen's fourth square.} Kb7 {The king, at his queen's
knight's second square.} 5. Kc5 {The king, at the adverse queen's bishop's fourth square.} Ka7
{The king, at his queen's rook's second square.} 6. Kb5 {The king, at the adverse queen's
knight's fourth square. (c) NOTE: In the present case he is obliged to place his king opposite
to yours, or absolutely to abandon the line.} Kb7 {The king, at his queen's knight's second
square.} 7. Rg7+ {The rook gives check, at the adverse king's knight's second square.} Kc8 {The
king, at his queen's bishop's square.} 8. Kc6 {The king, at the adverse queen's bishop's third
square.} Kd8 {The king, at his queen's square.} 9. Ra7 {The rook, at the adverse queen's rook's
second square.} Ke8 {The king, at his home.} 10. Kd6 {The king, at the adverse queen's third
square.} Kf8 {The king, at his bishop's square.} 11. Ke6 {The king, at the adverse king's third
square.} Kg8 {The king, at his knight's square.} 12. Kf6 {The king, at the adverse king's
bishop's third square.} Kh8 {The king, at his rook's square.} 13. Kg6 {The king, at the adverse
king's knight's third square.} Kg8 {The king, at his knight's square.} 14. Ra8# {The rook gives
check-mate. A GAME WON WITH A QUEEN AGAINST A ROOK AND A PAWN.} *
